
(verb
  :name "get-environment-variable" :std "R7RS-small"
  :library "core"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "rd"
  :signature "(get-environment-variable name) -> string or #f"
  :summary "R7RS §6.14 — the value of a named environment variable as a string, or #f if it is not set."
  :explanation "Reads one setting out of the environment the process was started in. Give it a name as a string; you get the value back as a string, or #f when there is nothing there. That #f is the whole ergonomics of the verb — it is false, so `or` and `if` handle the missing case with no extra test, and (or (get-environment-variable \"MOTOI_THEME\") \"default\") is the idiom you will write nine times out of ten. Motoi adds a security layer R7RS does not describe, and you need to know it before you trust an answer. The verb sits at the `personal-data` permission tier, so untrusted, external, sakura and animation dispatch tiers cannot call it at all. On top of that there is a DENYLIST: any name matching TOKEN, SECRET, KEY, PASSWORD, PASSWD, CREDENTIAL, COOKIE, SESSION, API, AUTH, BEARER, PRIVATE or SALT — case-insensitively, anywhere in the name — returns #f whether or not the variable is set, with PATH, MANPATH, INFOPATH and KRB5CCNAME explicitly exempted. That is deliberate and it is good, but it means #f is AMBIGUOUS: not-set and refused-to-tell-you are the same answer, and a variable named SOME_API_URL is unreadable purely because it contains API. If you control the naming, avoid those words. If you do not, the value is simply not reachable from Scheme."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(get-environment-variable \"MOTOI_NO_SUCH_VAR_XYZ\")" :note "#f — an unset variable answers false, which is exactly what you want for an `or` default.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(string? (get-environment-variable \"PATH\"))" :note "#t — a set variable answers a string. PATH is on the denylist's allow-list, so it reads through.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(or (get-environment-variable \"MOTOI_THEME\") \"default\")" :note "\"default\" — the standard idiom: #f for missing means `or` supplies the fallback with no extra test.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(get-environment-variable \"MOTOI_DEMO_TOKEN\")" :note "#f — and it stays #f even when the variable IS set, because the name contains TOKEN and the credential denylist refuses it.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(get-environment-variable \"SOME_API_URL\")" :note "#f — the denylist matches substrings, so a harmless URL is unreadable purely because API appears in its name.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (env-or name fallback)\n  (let ((v (get-environment-variable name)))\n    (if v v fallback)))\n(display (list (env-or \"MOTOI_NO_SUCH_VAR_XYZ\" \"fallback\") (string? (env-or \"PATH\" \"fallback\"))))\n(newline)" :note "(fallback #t) — one helper handles both cases; the fallback may be any value, not only a string.")
  )
  :caveats ("MOTOI SECURITY LAYER, not in R7RS: names matching /(TOKEN|SECRET|KEY|PASSWORD|PASSWD|CREDENTIAL|COOKIE|SESSION|API|AUTH|BEARER|PRIVATE|SALT)/i return #f even when set. PATH, MANPATH, INFOPATH and KRB5CCNAME are exempted." "The match is case-INSENSITIVE and is a SUBSTRING match, so MONKEY, SESSIONS and MY_API_URL are all refused." "#f is ambiguous: it means 'not set' OR 'name is denylisted'. You cannot tell which from the return value alone." "Tagged `personal-data` in the permission registry; untrusted, external, sakura and animation tiers cannot call it." "Values are always strings. Use string->number before arithmetic and do not assume \"0\" is false — it is a non-empty string and therefore truthy." "The contents are host-dependent. Never assert on a specific value in a test or a reference example.")
  :usecases ("Reading an optional setting with a compiled-in default, via `or`." "Letting an operator switch on debug output for one session without editing source." "Locating a data directory that differs per machine." "Checking at startup which of a set of required settings are missing, and reporting them all at once.")
  :related ("get-environment-variables" "command-line" "features" "string->number" "or")
  :learn (:concept "#f as the missing-value answer is a design choice that makes the common case — 'use this, or a default' — a single expression." :prerequisites ("or" "if" "string?") :progression "Read a variable that is not set and see #f. Wrap it in `or` with a default. Read PATH and see a real string come back. Then read a name containing TOKEN and notice #f again, for a completely different reason.")
  :provenance "R7RS §6.14; implementation lib/base/r7rs-small.js:1452, permission tier 'personal-data', with the CRED_KEY_RE credential denylist added by Priya 2026-07-17 (Motoi-own, not in R7RS); measured against the live engine 2026-08-02 using dummy variables only; reference record added 2026-08-02"
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (setting name default)\n  (let ((v (get-environment-variable name))) (if v v default)))\n(display (list (setting \"MOTOI_NO_SUCH_VAR_XYZ\" \"quiet\") (string? (setting \"PATH\" \"quiet\"))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (list (get-environment-variable \"MOTOI_NO_SUCH_VAR_XYZ\") (get-environment-variable \"MOTOI_DEMO_TOKEN\")))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'unset-and-scrubbed-look-alike (eq? (get-environment-variable \"MOTOI_NO_SUCH_VAR_XYZ\") (get-environment-variable \"MOTOI_DEMO_TOKEN\"))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A setting must differ per machine — a data directory, a theme, a verbosity level. Candidates: a config file the program reads at startup (structured and typed, but it is another file to find, parse and keep in sync), a command-line flag (visible and per-run, but must be retyped on every invocation), or an environment variable."
     :explanation "eng: one helper covers set and unset, and the answer for PATH really is a string. math: two different reasons for absence produce the identical answer. cs: eq? on the two #f results is #t, which is the ambiguity stated as an equation — the program cannot distinguish them."
     :meta "Reach for get-environment-variable and NOT a command-line flag when the setting belongs to the MACHINE or the session rather than to this run: you set it once in a shell profile and every invocation inherits it. Reach for a flag when the setting belongs to this run — the shell history then records what you actually asked for. And reach for a config file the moment the settings have structure, because environment variables are a flat namespace of strings and stay that way.")
    (:eng "One helper at the boundary means the rest of the program never sees #f and never re-checks." :math "Two distinct causes mapping to one value is information loss, and it is irreversible at the call site." :cs "Ambient configuration is convenient and untyped; the type discipline has to be added by you, at the edge."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "A value that lives outside the program, is chosen by whoever runs it, and may simply not be there."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "core" :code "(display (string? (get-environment-variable \"PATH\")))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "a preference set once on a machine and inherited by everything on it" :code "(display (if (get-environment-variable \"MOTOI_NO_SUCH_VAR_XYZ\") 'configured 'defaulted))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "a partial function, undefined at some points of its domain" :code "(display (list 'absent (get-environment-variable \"MOTOI_NO_SUCH_VAR_XYZ\")))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "A machine-wide preference is inherited by every program that starts there without any of them asking for it — the value flows down rather than being passed across. A partial function is one that has no value at some inputs, and every language has to decide what to do at those points: raise, return a sentinel, or return an option type. This verb picks the sentinel, and picks #f specifically because #f is the one value Scheme's conditionals already treat as absence."
     :meta "Choosing #f as the sentinel is what makes `or` work as a defaulting operator, and it is also why the denylist result is indistinguishable from the missing result. The same property that gives you the nice idiom takes away the ability to tell two failures apart — a trade the implementation made on purpose and you should know you inherited."
    )
    (:eng "A machine preference inherited by every process is configuration that nobody has to remember to pass." :math "A partial function needs a total representation; the sentinel makes it total at the cost of collapsing the reasons." :cs "#f-as-absent versus an option type: the first composes with `or`, the second preserves why."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (list (string? (get-environment-variable \"PATH\")) (get-environment-variable \"MOTOI_NO_SUCH_VAR_XYZ\")))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (list 'same-answer (get-environment-variable \"MOTOI_DEMO_SECRET\") (get-environment-variable \"MOTOI_ABSENT_VAR\")))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'case-insensitive (get-environment-variable \"motoi_demo_token\") (get-environment-variable \"MOTOI_DEMO_TOKEN\")))\n(newline)"
     :claim "The result is a string when the variable is set and readable, and #f otherwise — where 'otherwise' covers both an unset variable and a name the credential denylist refuses, case-insensitively."
     :invariant "get-environment-variable(n) ∈ strings ∪ {#f}; and for any n matching the credential pattern and not in the allow-list, get-environment-variable(n) = #f regardless of the environment"
     :meta "The second and third programs establish the part that is Motoi-specific and not in R7RS at all. Both spellings of a TOKEN-bearing name answer #f, so the denylist is not defeated by lowercasing, and a denylisted name is indistinguishable from a name that was simply never set. What would falsify the claim: a denylisted variable returning its value, or a set, non-denylisted variable returning #f. Neither happens. Note that these programs deliberately use invented variable names — probing a REAL credential variable would print a secret, which is the exact thing the denylist exists to prevent."
    )
    (:eng "Two values in the codomain, strings and #f, is the entire type of this verb." :math "The denylist makes the function constant on a whole subset of its domain, independent of the environment." :cs "Case-insensitive substring denylisting is coarse and errs toward refusal, which is the correct direction for a secrets filter."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (env-or name fallback)\n  (let ((v (get-environment-variable name))) (if v v fallback)))\n(display (list (env-or \"MOTOI_A\" 1) (env-or \"MOTOI_B\" 'sym) (string? (env-or \"PATH\" 'sym))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (all-set? names)\n  (let loop ((ns names) (missing '()))\n    (cond ((null? ns) (reverse missing))\n          ((get-environment-variable (car ns)) (loop (cdr ns) missing))\n          (else (loop (cdr ns) (cons (car ns) missing))))))\n(display (all-set? (list \"PATH\" \"MOTOI_NO_SUCH_VAR_XYZ\")))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (layered name default)\n  (let ((v (get-environment-variable name)))\n    (cond ((not v) (list 'default default))\n          ((string=? v \"\") (list 'default default))\n          (else (list 'from-env name)))))\n(display (list (layered \"MOTOI_NO_SUCH_VAR_XYZ\" 'd) (layered \"PATH\" 'd)))\n(newline)"
     :base "get-environment-variable answers a string or #f for one named setting."
     :emergent "eng: a two-line env-or lifts the fallback out of the string type entirely — the default may be a number or a symbol, so the setting arrives already typed. math: folding the read across a LIST of required names produces the set of missing ones in one pass, which is a startup precondition check rather than a lookup. cs: treating the empty string as absent too collapses the two ways a setting can be unhelpful, which is what an operator who typed `MOTOI_THEME=` actually meant."
     :meta "The composition to keep is the second. Reading settings one at a time means discovering the gaps one crash at a time; reading the required set at startup and printing every missing name at once is the difference between a program that is annoying to configure and one that is not. Build env-or first, build the required-set check on top of it, and do both at the edge of the program so nothing downstream ever sees a #f."
    )
    (:eng "Checking every requirement at startup beats discovering them one failure at a time." :math "The missing set is the preimage of #f over the required names — computed in one pass." :cs "Normalising empty-string to absent is a policy decision; make it once, at the boundary, not at every read."))
)

(verb
  :name "get-environment-variables" :std "R7RS-small"
  :library "core"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "rd"
  :signature "(get-environment-variables) -> list of (name value) lists"
  :summary "R7RS §6.14 — every readable environment variable, as a list of two-element (name value) lists."
  :explanation "The whole environment at once, rather than one name at a time. Every entry is a two-element LIST whose car is the variable's name and whose cadr is its value, both strings. Read that sentence again if you have used this verb in another Scheme, because R7RS specifies an ALIST of dotted pairs — (name . value) — and Motoi returns (name value). The difference is invisible to `assoc`, which finds the entry either way, and fatal to the line after it: (cdr entry) is the LIST (\"…\") here, not the string, so the accessor you want is `cadr`. Portable code that does (cdr (assoc name (get-environment-variables))) will silently get a one-element list and then fail somewhere else. The same credential denylist that governs get-environment-variable governs this list too, and here it is subtractive rather than falsifying: variables whose names match TOKEN, SECRET, KEY, API, AUTH and the rest are simply ABSENT from the result, so you cannot tell from this list whether they exist. Because of that, and because the whole thing is host-dependent, treat the return value as something to search, count and convert — never as something to print, compare against a fixture, or assert a length on."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(list? (get-environment-variables))" :note "#t — a plain list, so every list operation applies with no unwrapping.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(> (length (get-environment-variables)) 0)" :note "#t — non-empty in any normal shell; the exact count is host-dependent and must never be asserted.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(length (car (get-environment-variables)))" :note "2 — each entry is a two-element LIST, not a dotted pair. R7RS specifies a dotted pair here.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((e (assoc \"PATH\" (get-environment-variables)))) (list (string? (cdr e)) (string? (cadr e))))" :note "(#f #t) — the trap in one line: cdr gives a LIST, cadr gives the value. Portable code that uses cdr breaks here.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let loop ((xs (get-environment-variables))) (if (null? xs) 'all-pairs-of-two (if (= 2 (length (car xs))) (loop (cdr xs)) 'ragged)))" :note "all-pairs-of-two — the shape is uniform across every entry, so one accessor works for all of them.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (env-ref name)\n  (let ((hit (assoc name (get-environment-variables))))\n    (if hit (cadr hit) #f)))\n(display (list (string? (env-ref \"PATH\")) (env-ref \"MOTOI_NO_SUCH_VAR_XYZ\")))\n(newline)" :note "(#t #f) — rebuilding get-environment-variable from the list; note the cadr, and note that a miss must be turned into #f by hand.")
  )
  :caveats ("DIVERGES FROM R7RS: entries are two-element LISTS (name value), not the dotted pairs (name . value) the standard specifies. (cdr entry) is a list; use (cadr entry) for the value." "assoc still works, which is what makes the divergence dangerous — the lookup succeeds and only the accessor after it is wrong." "The credential denylist REMOVES entries: any name matching TOKEN, SECRET, KEY, PASSWORD, PASSWD, CREDENTIAL, COOKIE, SESSION, API, AUTH, BEARER, PRIVATE or SALT is absent, so this list is not the real environment. PATH, MANPATH, INFOPATH and KRB5CCNAME are exempted and do appear." "Tagged `personal-data`; untrusted, external, sakura and animation tiers cannot call it." "The length and contents are host-dependent. Never assert a count, never print the whole thing into a log or a transcript." "Each call rebuilds the list, so it is never eq? to a previous result.")
  :usecases ("Listing which of a set of expected settings are actually present." "Converting the environment into a hash table once, then doing many fast lookups." "Diffing the environment against a recorded manifest to see what a wrapper script changed." "Counting or filtering settings by name prefix, which one-at-a-time reads cannot do.")
  :related ("get-environment-variable" "command-line" "features" "assoc" "hash-table-set!")
  :learn (:concept "An association list is a lookup table made of pairs, and the exact SHAPE of each entry — dotted pair or two-element list — determines which accessor is correct." :prerequisites ("assoc" "car" "cadr" "length") :progression "Confirm it is a list. Take one entry and measure its length — 2, not a dotted pair. Pull a value out with cadr and watch cdr give you the wrong thing. Then rebuild get-environment-variable from it, and you have understood the shape.")
  :provenance "R7RS §6.14; implementation lib/base/r7rs-small.js:1458 — returns [k, v] arrays (two-element lists), NOT the dotted pairs R7RS specifies; credential denylist filters entries out (Motoi-own, Priya 2026-07-17); measured against the live engine 2026-08-02; reference record added 2026-08-02"
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(display (list 'count-positive (> (length (get-environment-variables)) 0)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (list 'entry-length (length (car (get-environment-variables)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (names) (map car (get-environment-variables)))\n(display (list 'all-strings (let loop ((xs (names))) (if (null? xs) #t (and (string? (car xs)) (loop (cdr xs)))))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A startup routine needs to know which of a dozen expected settings are present. Candidates: call get-environment-variable a dozen times (simple, and it re-reads the environment a dozen times, and it cannot answer 'what else is set'), grab the whole environment once and search it, or shell out to `env` and parse the text (works and drags a subprocess and a parser into a question that needed neither)."
     :explanation "eng: the list is non-empty in any real shell, so a length test is safe. math: every entry has length 2 — the fact that decides which accessor is correct. cs: mapping car over the entries gives the NAMES, which is a question one-at-a-time reads cannot answer at all."
     :meta "Reach for get-environment-variables and NOT repeated single reads when the question is about the environment AS A SET — what is present, what is missing, what matches a prefix. Reach for the single read when you know the name: it is one call, it is clearer at the point of use, and it does not tempt you into printing the whole environment somewhere it will be logged.")
    (:eng "Reading once and searching beats reading many times, and it answers questions the many-reads version cannot." :math "Uniform entry length is the precondition that lets one accessor serve every entry." :cs "The set of NAMES is only available from the bulk read; that is the capability difference, not a performance one."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "The whole context a program was handed, presented as a table you can search rather than a question you must already know how to ask."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "core" :code "(display (list? (get-environment-variables)))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "the full settings panel rather than one dial" :code "(display (list 'entries (> (length (get-environment-variables)) 0)))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "a finite function given as its graph, the set of input-output pairs" :code "(display (list 'shape (length (car (get-environment-variables)))))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "One dial tells you one setting and the panel tells you the configuration. A finite function can be given by a RULE or by its GRAPH — the explicit set of (input, output) pairs — and the graph lets you ask questions the rule cannot answer without search: how many inputs are defined, which ones, what is the range. get-environment-variables is the graph; get-environment-variable is the rule."
     :meta "The graph is strictly more informative than pointwise evaluation, and strictly more dangerous to handle: it is bigger, it is host-specific, and it contains everything, including things you did not mean to look at. That is exactly why the credential denylist removes entries from it rather than merely blanking them."
    )
    (:eng "A panel shows what you did not know to ask about; a dial only answers the question you brought." :math "A function given as its graph exposes its domain; given as a rule, the domain must be discovered." :cs "Enumerable configuration enables auditing and diffing — and requires a filter, because enumeration exposes everything."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (let ((e (assoc \"PATH\" (get-environment-variables)))) (list 'pair (pair? e) 'cdr-string (string? (cdr e)) 'cadr-string (string? (cadr e)))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (list 'uniform (let loop ((xs (get-environment-variables))) (if (null? xs) #t (and (= 2 (length (car xs))) (loop (cdr xs)))))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (find-key k)\n  (let loop ((xs (get-environment-variables)))\n    (cond ((null? xs) #f) ((string=? (car (car xs)) k) #t) (else (loop (cdr xs))))))\n(display (list 'path (find-key \"PATH\") 'nonexistent (find-key \"MOTOI_NO_SUCH_VAR_XYZ\")))\n(newline)"
     :claim "Every entry is a two-element list of strings — uniformly, across the whole result — so assoc finds entries correctly and cdr does NOT yield the value; cadr does."
     :invariant "For every entry e in get-environment-variables(): pair?(e) = #t, length(e) = 2, string?(car e) = #t, string?(cadr e) = #t, and string?(cdr e) = #f"
     :meta "The first program is the whole reason this record exists. `pair` is #t and `cdr-string` is #f in the same breath: the entry really is a pair, so assoc works and every type check you would think to write passes, and the value still is not where R7RS says it is. A program ported from another Scheme fails not at the lookup but one line later, with a list where a string was expected. What would falsify the claim: string?(cdr e) answering #t, which is what the standard requires and what this implementation does not do."
    )
    (:eng "The failure lands one line after the mistake, which is the most expensive place for a failure to land." :math "length 2 and dotted-pair-ness are different properties; only one of them is what the standard specified." :cs "assoc succeeding on both shapes is why this divergence survives a code review — the lookup is not the broken part."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (env-alist)\n  (map (lambda (e) (cons (car e) (cadr e))) (get-environment-variables)))\n(display (list 'cdr-is-now-a-string (string? (cdr (assoc \"PATH\" (env-alist))))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(display (list 'names-match-entries (= (length (map car (get-environment-variables))) (length (get-environment-variables)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (env-hash)\n  (let ((h (make-hash-table)))\n    (for-each (lambda (e) (hash-table-set! h (car e) (cadr e))) (get-environment-variables))\n    h))\n(display (list (string? (hash-table-ref/default (env-hash) \"PATH\" #f)) (hash-table-ref/default (env-hash) \"MOTOI_NO_SUCH_VAR_XYZ\" 'missing)))\n(newline)"
     :base "get-environment-variables hands back every readable setting as a two-element list."
     :emergent "eng: one map with cons REPAIRS the shape — after env-alist the result is the dotted alist R7RS describes, and portable code that uses cdr works again. math: names and entries have the same count, so mapping car is a bijection onto the name set and the list can be treated as a finite function. cs: pouring the entries into a hash table trades a linear scan per lookup for one pass and constant-time reads, with a default for the miss."
     :meta "The composition to keep is the first, and it is one line. If you are writing code that might ever run on another Scheme, convert at the boundary — (map (lambda (e) (cons (car e) (cadr e))) …) — and everything downstream is portable, including code you copied out of a book. The hash-table version is the right move when you are doing many lookups, but do the shape repair first; a hash table built from the wrong accessor is a bug that has been made fast."
    )
    (:eng "Repair the shape once at the boundary and every downstream reader becomes portable." :math "Equal cardinality of names and entries is what licenses treating the list as a function." :cs "Convert a scan to a hash when lookups are many — but fix the representation before you optimise the access."))
)
